<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/music-history-eras">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Music history is organised into eras — <strong>Baroque, Classical, Romantic, Modern</strong> —
    each with characteristic instruments, forms and social settings. The eras are real (you can
    hear the difference) but also constructions: they describe European art music, while the
    fuller story includes every tradition playing in parallel. For the IGCSE Listening paper the
    eras are anchored on focus works — Vivaldi and Handel for the Baroque, Mozart for the
    Classical, Smetana for the Romantic — and the fastest way to hear an era is to know its
    vocabulary: what a ritornello is, why a continuo matters, what a symphonic poem does. Read
    the eras as chapters, not the whole book — each one is a conversation between the music, the
    technology of the instruments and the society paying for it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four eras and the social forces inside them" widget="equationStep" config={erasCfg} />

  <h2>Baroque: doctrine, dance and the birth of the orchestra</h2>
  <p>
    The Baroque (roughly 1600–1750) runs on <em>affect</em> — each movement projects one
    emotion, sustained by a motor rhythm and, underneath everything, the <strong>basso
    continuo</strong>: a bass line instrument (cello, bassoon) with a chordal partner
    (harpsichord or organ) filling in the harmony from figures, improvising the accompaniment
    in real time. Vivaldi's <em>Spring</em>, first movement (Allegro, in E major, from the
    collection <em>Il cimento dell'armonia e dell'inventione</em>, 1725), shows the era's
    favourite architecture — <strong>ritornello form</strong>: the full orchestra's catchy
    opening tutti returns like a refrain while the solo violin plays contrasting episodes
    drawn from the sonnet printed with the score — birdsong in high repeated trills, murmuring
    streams in running semiquavers, then a thunderstorm in tremolo strings and scurrying
    scales. The other great concerto type is the <strong>concerto grosso</strong>:
    Handel's set of twelve <em>Grand Concertos</em>, Op. 6 (composed in about a month in
    autumn 1739, published in London in 1740), pits a small <strong>concertino</strong> of two
    violins and cello against the full
    <strong>ripieno</strong> string band; No. 5 in D major (HWV 323) opens with one of
    Handel's most majestic French overtures — slow, dotted, ceremonial — before moving through
    dance-derived movements. The solo concerto (one protagonist) and concerto grosso (a small
    team) are the era's two competing dramatic engines. Performance practice completes the
    sound-world: smaller string sections, gut strings and natural trumpets, terraced dynamics
    (loud tutti, quiet solos, few graduations), and ornamentation — trills, appoggiaturas,
    improvised embellishments on repeats — that the notation assumes the performer will add.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the basso continuo is just a harpsichord playing chords."
    It is a partnership of at least two players — a bass instrument carrying the written line
    and a chordal instrument <em>realising</em> the harmony, un-notated, from figures — and it
    underpins nearly all Baroque ensemble music, from opera recitative to concerto. Miss it
    and the texture's motor, the reason Baroque music moves the way it does, disappears; it
    is also the clearest audible marker distinguishing a historically informed performance
    from a modern-orchestra one.
  </p>
  <h2>Classical: conversation, proportion and the sonata</h2>
  <p>
    The Classical style (roughly 1750–1820) trades Baroque density for clarity: periodic
    phrasing (two- and four-bar questions answered), Alberti-bass accompaniments, gradual
    dynamics (<em>crescendo</em> now possible with the flexing early pianoforte), and sonata
    form as the era's great engine — exposition, development, recapitulation — driven by the
    drama of moving between keys. Its social setting changed too: the public concert emerged,
    and music left the court chamber for ticket-buying audiences. Chamber music — works for
    three to six players, one per part, without conductor — became the style's purest
    laboratory: the string quartet, the piano trio, and hybrids like Mozart's <strong>Quintet
    for piano and winds in E flat major, K. 452</strong> (piano, oboe, clarinet, horn,
    bassoon). Completed in March 1784 and premiered to acclaim at the Burgtheater on 1 April,
    it made Mozart so proud that he wrote to his father on 10 April that he considered it the
    finest work he had ever composed; Beethoven found the scoring compelling enough to reuse
    it for his own Quintet, Op. 16. Its three movements — a Largo introduction flowing into a
    sonata-form Allegro moderato, a songful Larghetto, and a Rondo finale — treat the wind
    quartet as equals of the piano rather than accompanists: each instrument takes the tune in
    turn, and the slow movement's woodwind duetting over the piano's figures is chamber
    writing of the highest finish. The first movement blends concerto-like dialogue with
    chamber equality: the piano leads, the winds answer, and no instrument is mere
    accompaniment — Classical form as conversation among equals.
  </p>

  <h2>Romantic: programme, nation and the single movement</h2>
  <p>
    The Romantic era (roughly 1820–1900) redirected music's ambition: emotion over proportion,
    literature and painting as partners, nationalism, virtuosity, and orchestras swollen to
    Wagnerian size. Its emblematic genre was the <strong>single-movement orchestral
    work</strong> — the symphonic poem, named and pioneered by Liszt (his <em>Les Préludes</em>,
    premiered in 1854, was the first work he called one) — and its
    emblematic impulse was <strong>programme music</strong>: instrumental music that follows a
    narrative or scene. Berlioz's <em>Symphonie fantastique</em> (1830) had already attached a
    story and a returning <em>idée fixe</em> to a symphony; Smetana went further and built a
    whole national epic. <strong>Vltava</strong> (composed November 1874, by which time Smetana
    was completely deaf) is the second of six symphonic poems in <em>Má vlast</em>
    ("My Homeland"): one continuous movement following the river from its two springs through
    a forest hunt, a rustic wedding with a polka, moonlit water nymphs, and the St John's
    Rapids, before the famous broad theme opens out — and the closing bars quote the
    Vyšehrad motif from the cycle's first poem, tying the river to Prague's fortress rock.
    The main theme's own journey is a lesson in Romantic expression: it is first heard
    darkly in the minor, then opens into radiant major as the river broadens toward Prague —
    the same tune, re-coloured by mode, doing the narrative work that words would do in a
    song. The theme itself has a second life: it belongs to a pan-European folk family descending from
    the Renaissance tune "La Mantovana" — the same ancestry as Israel's anthem "Hatikvah" — a
    reminder that "national" melodies are often the whole region's common property. The
    Romantic trends the syllabus names are all audible in it: programme content, national
    subject matter, richer orchestration, expressive (not terraced) dynamics, and the
    composer as storyteller — and the genre ran on through Richard Strauss's tone poems
    (<em>Don Juan</em>, 1889) and beyond, into the twentieth century's film scores, which are
    programme music by descent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "nationalist music means quoting folk songs." Some Czech
    critics complained precisely that Smetana used no genuine folk material — but his reply
    was the point: he composed <em>original</em> themes in a folk-like idiom, and let the
    subject, the Czech titles and the polka rhythms do the national work. Romantic nationalism
    was usually a style of musical behaviour (dances, modes, landscape painting, patriotic
    programmes) rather than a library of borrowed melodies — which is why Vltava sounds
    Czech without quoting a single Czech tune.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "programme music tells the story in order, like a film
    soundtrack, so you must be able to follow the plot." Programmes are frames, not
    subtitles: they set the mood and justify the structure (Vltava's episodes really do
    follow the river downstream, but <em>Symphonie fantastique</em>'s programme was revised
    and partly abandoned by Berlioz), and a listener who has never read either programme can
    still hear the form. The exam skill is describing the musical means — rhythm, orchestral
    colour, key, texture — that create the effect, not guessing the narrative.
  </p>

  <h2>Patronage, technology and the canon: how the eras really changed</h2>
  <p>
    Behind the style changes stand three forces worth tracking across every era. Instruments
    shifted what was writable: valve horns and the sustaining pianoforte enabled Romantic
    expressiveness; the electric guitar, synthesiser and drum machine later created genres,
    not just sounds. Patronage shaped the product: church → court → public concert → record
    label → streaming, and who pays decides what gets written — Bach wrote weekly cantatas
    for a town's churches, Mozart for subscribers, Tchaikovsky for a tsar's patron. Recording
    (from 1877) then made music repeatable, portable and global: performances frozen for
    comparison, local styles cross-pollinated within a decade. The canon, meanwhile, is
    edited continually — and assuming it was always there is the era-student's most common
    error. Bach was considered old-fashioned in his lifetime and revived by
    Mendelssohn's 1829 performance of the <em>St Matthew Passion</em>, a century after his
    death; Vivaldi was a footnote until the twentieth century; Clara Schumann was a
    celebrated composer-pianist written out of the repertoire and written back in. The
    twentieth century — Schoenberg's atonality, Stravinsky's <em>Rite</em> riot of 1913,
    jazz, electronics, minimalism, global fusion — multiplied "classical" into one stream
    among many. The eras are chapters, continuously re-edited by the present.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: focus works and the vocabulary of three eras" widget="quiz" config={erasQuizCfg} />

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The periodisation problem.</strong> The textbook dates are conventions, and the
    edges are famously soft: Handel and Bach died in the 1750s still writing Baroque music
    while the galant style circled them; Beethoven's career spans both sides of the Classical
    door and opened the Romantic one (the <em>Eroica</em>, 1804, is claimed by both eras);
    "reform" operas and <em>Sturm und Drang</em> crossings blur every boundary. Treat the
    dates as signposts toward clusters of features — continuo, texture, dynamics, form,
    instrumentation — not as walls; examiners reward feature-spotting over date-reciting.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Historically informed performance.</strong> Since the 1970s a whole discipline has
    asked how this music sounded to its first audiences: gut strings, lower pitches, natural
    trumpets and horns, smaller forces, continuo realisations, singers' ornamentation, dance
    tempos — compare a 1960s symphonic <em>Spring</em> with a one-to-a-part period recording
    and you hear two different works. Tempos alone split the field: "Allegro" meant something
    different on Baroque instruments, at Baroque pitch, in Baroque halls, and the metronome
    did not exist until 1815. The movement's deeper claim is that performance
    decisions are interpretive arguments, and the debates (how much ornament? how fast is
    "Allegro"?) remain unresolved because the evidence underdetermines the answers — which is
    why "wider listening" is itself a research method.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The wider map.</strong> The era labels cover European art music while the rest of
    the world's traditions ran on their own clocks: the rāga-tāla system, the maqām tradition
    and the great court musics of the Mughal, Qing and Ottoman worlds were flourishing across
    the very years labelled "Baroque", with their own theory, notation and patrons — the
    seventeenth century that Europe calls the age of Monteverdi was simultaneously an age of
    dhrupad masters, Ottoman mehterhane bands and Persian art song. Global
    musicology now reads those traditions in parallel rather than as backdrop — and the
    syllabus itself mirrors the shift, pairing the western classical areas of study with
    tango, Hindustani and Arab ensembles. The expert's habit of mind: date the style, name
    the features, and then ask whose music the timeline is quietly leaving out.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Grout & Palisca, A History of Western Music; Taruskin, The Oxford History of Western Music; recordings as primary sources.</p>
